<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Hanifa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">699–767</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Abū Ḥanīfa, Nu'man</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Hanifa was a Muslim scholar, jurist and theologian, the founder and eponym of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> jurisprudence. After studying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> he succeeded his teacher Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman as the leading authority on Islamic law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, and his school was distinguished by its use of analogy (qiyas), juristic preference (istihsan) and personal judgement (ra'y). He refused the post of chief judge offered by the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur in 763, was imprisoned and tortured, and died in prison in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 767. Through his students Abu Yusuf and Muhammad al-Shaybani his school spread, and it remains the most widely practised of the Sunni schools.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In these two centuries they built the schools of law named after <b>Abu Hanifa</b>, Malik, al-Shafi’i and Ahmad ibn Hanbal.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>The caliph al-Mansur then had supporters such as <b>Abu Hanifa</b> imprisoned.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-14">14 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
